 [["B2", 0.384], ["B1", 0.362], ["B3", 0.22]]}, {"top": [["B3", 0.328], ["B2", 0.319], ["B1", 0.301]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No acute life-threatening instability detected.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch (B2) posterior 0.319 < 0.75. Dangerous alternative B1 (posterior 0.301 >= 0.10, danger 0.7) remains unresolved. Depth limit (3) not reached (current level 1)."}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.45, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3283594326473723, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Alpha cell tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Beta cell destruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Beta cell tumor"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Hypercortisolism"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Insulin resistance"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'pica', 'Secondary hypercortisolism', 'Autoimmunity', 'hirsutism', 'Primary hypercortisolism', 'Reduced pancreatic beta cells', 'Beta-cell dysfunction', 'skin changes'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B3", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.434}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B3", "label": "Autoimmune Beta Cell Destruction", "posterior": 0.328, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 2}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 2, "disconfirming": 2, "last_disconfirming_digest": "The absence of classic type 1 diabetes symptoms weakly against autoimmune beta cell destruction (B3) as the primary mec…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "refute"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B1", "B4"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=75%, source=both]\n\nInsulin Resistance Syndromes vs Autoimmune Beta Cell Destruction:\n  Favours first:  abnormal circulating fatty-acid concentration, abnormal circulating lipid concentration, abnormal oral glucose tolerance, abnormal salivary gland morphology, abnormality of body weight\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, autosomal dominant inheritance, bed-wetting in children, blurred vision, confusion\n\nInsulin Resistance Syndromes vs Hypercortisolism-Related Disorders:\n  Favours first:  abnormal circulating fatty-acid concentration, abnormal circulating lipid concentration, abnormal oral glucose tolerance, abnormal salivary gland morphology, abnormality of body weight\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nAutoimmune Beta Cell Destruction vs Hypercortisolism-Related Disorders:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, bed-wetting in children, blurred vision, confusion, darkened skin patches\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nRelated to Insulin Resistance Syndromes: diabetes mellitus (disease)\n\nRelated to Autoimmune Beta Cell Destruction: autoimmune disease of endocrine system, autoimmune polyendocrine, diabetes mellitus (disease)\n\nRelated to Hypercortisolism-Related Disorders: alcohol amnestic disorder, alcohol sensitivity, acute, alcoholic cardiomyopathy\n\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: Islet cell tumours. > History and Physical\n  Insulinoma presents with hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia in patients. The disease is manifested as the Whipple triad. This includes symptoms of low blood glucose, along with the symptoms of hypoglycemia\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.71):]\n  Source: Approach to the Patient: Insulinoma. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Insulinoma is a rare functional neuroendocrine tumor arising from pancreatic islet cells. Most cases are sporadic, although some are linked to multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1) syndrome. Thes\n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.72):]\n  Source: Islet cell tumours. > History and Physical\n  Insulinoma presents with hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia in patients. The disease is manifested as the Whipple triad. This includes symptoms of low blood glucose, along with the symptoms of hypoglycemia\n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.76):]\n  Source: A review of cutaneous manifestations within glucagonoma syndrome: necrolytic migratory erythema. > Continuing Education Activity\n  Necrolytic migratory erythema (NME) is a characteristic skin rash most often associated with glucagonoma, an alpha-cell tumor of the pancreatic islets. It is usually seen as a part of the glucagonoma \n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  malaise → Systemic symptoms of hypercortisolism (Cushing syndrome) → Hypercortisolism-Related Disorders\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Investigate for hypercortisolism signs such as elevated cortisol levels, skin changes (e.g., striae), and abdominal distention despite the absence of fever\n  and a painful rash → Cutaneous manifestations of hyperglycemia (e.g., necrobiosis lipoidica, diabetic dermopathy) → Endocrine Tumors with Hyperglycemic and Cutaneous Features\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate the rash for features specific to hyperglycemia-induced dermatoses and correlate with glucose metabolism markers\n  The patient states that his symptoms have been worsening over the past week → Progressive autoimmune destruction of beta cells leading to type 1 diabetes mellitus → Autoimmune Beta Cell Destruction\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Test for beta cell autoantibodies (e.g., GAD65, IA-2) to confirm autoimmune etiology of progressive symptoms\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
